"Internal Error ()\nciteturn65view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0921452620307389\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOrigin of the X-ray magnetic circular dichroism at the L-edges of the rare-earths in RxR1-x'Al2 systems (https://journals.iucr.org/s/issues/2009/03/00/ot5597/ot5597.pdf)\nciteturn65view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://journals.iucr.org/s/issues/2009/03/00/ot5597/ot5597.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 695\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Experimental and theoretical analyses of PrAl2 and NdAl2 composite for use as an active magnetic regenerator (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/224493159_Experimental_and_theoretical_analyses_of_PrAl2_and_NdAl2_composite_for_use_as_an_active_magnetic_regenerator)\nciteturn65view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/224493159_Experimental_and_theoretical_analyses_of_PrAl2_and_NdAl2_composite_for_use_as_an_active_magnetic_regenerator\",\"lineno\":430}); Total lines: 1434\nL76: cite20†Temperature dependence of Δ S mag in Nd Al 2 for a magnetic field change from 0 to 5 T calculated for the easy magnetic direction ⟨001⟩ and measured in a polycrystalline sample. The solid line represents the theoretical results using the model parameters given and the dotted line was calculated using the CEF and exchange parameters from Ref. 7. The open circles show the experimental data. …  L77: \nL78: Figures - uploaded by cite3†Alexandre Magnus Gomes Carvalho L79: \nL80: Author content\nL81: All figure content in this area was uploaded by Alexandre Magnus Gomes Carvalho\nL82: \nL83: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL84: \nL85: cite126†Image: ResearchGate Logo L86: \nL87: Discover the world's research\nL88: \nL89:   * 25+ million members\nL90:   * 160+ million publication pages\nL91:   * 2.3+ billion citations\nL92: \nL93: cite21†Join for free L94: \nL95: Public Full-text 1\nL96: \nL97: Content uploaded by cite3†Alexandre Magnus Gomes Carvalho L98: \nL99: Author content\nL100: \nL101: All content in this area was uploaded by Alexandre Magnus Gomes Carvalho on Mar 15, 2016\nL102: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL103: \nL104: Experimental and theoretical analyses of PrAl 2 and NdAl 2 composite\nL105: \nL106: for use as an active magnetic regenerator\nL107: \nL108: A. Magnus G. Carvalho,a 兲 J. C. P. Campoy, A. A. Coelho, E. J. R. Plaza, and S. Gama\nL109: \nL110: Instituto de Física “Gleb W ataghin,” Universidade Estadual de Campinas (UNICAMP),\nL111: \nL112: Caixa Postal 6165, Campinas 13 083 970, Sao Paulo, Brazil\nL113: \nL114: P. J. von Ranke\nL115: \nL116: Departamento de Eletrônica Quântica (DEQ), Universidade do Estado do Rio de Janeiro,\nL117: Instituto de Fisica (IF), Rua São Francisco Xavier, 524, 20550-013, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\nL118: \nL119: 共 Received 5 August 2004; accepted 27 January 2005; published online 5 April 2005 兲\nL120: \nL121: W e report the theoretical and experimental investigations on the magnetocaloric effect in the PrAl 2\nL122: \nL123: and NdAl 2 compounds and a composite of these compounds for use as an active magnetic\nL124: \nL125: regenerator. The theoretical calculations were performed considering the crystalline electrical ﬁeld\nL126: anisotropy and the magnetocaloric potentials were calculated in the three main crystallographic\nL127: \nL128: directions. The experimental data, obtained for the polycrystalline samples, are in good agreement\nL129: \nL130: with the theoretical results. Also, an optimum molar fraction of the PrAl 2 and NdAl 2 composite was\nL131: \nL132: determined theoretically and experimentally and discussed in the framework of the optimum\nL133: \nL134: regeneration Ericsson cycle. © 2005 American Institute of Physics.关 DOI: 10.1063/1.1876575 兴\nL135: \nL136: INTRODUCTION\nL137: Since Brown 1 described a practical near-room-\nL138: \nL139: temperature magnetic refrigerator using magnetocaloric ef-\nL140: \nL141: fect 共 MCE 兲, and the discovery of the giant MCE in\nL142: \nL143: Gd 5 共 Si 2 Ge 2 兲共 Ref. 2 兲 and recently in MnFeP 0.45 As 0.55 共 Ref.\nL144: \nL145: 3 兲 and MnAs 1−x Sb x,4,5 the interest in this research area has\nL146: \nL147: been considerably enhanced. Magnetic refrigeration is based\nL148: \nL149: on the MCE, the ability of magnetic materials to heat up\nL150: \nL151: when they are magnetized, and cool down when removed\nL152: from the magnetic ﬁeld in an adiabatic process. The two\nL153: \nL154: thermodynamic quantities that characterize the MCE are\nL155: \nL156: ⌬S mag 共 the isothermal magnetic entropy change 兲 and ⌬T ad\nL157: \nL158: 共 the adiabatic temperature change 兲, which are observed upon\nL159: \nL160: changes in the external magnetic ﬁeld. Improving in mag-\nL161: \nL162: netic refrigeration requires investigations on the microscopic\nL163: \nL164: mechanisms that are responsible for an increase in these two\nL165: \nL166: thermodynamic quantities, and this subject is of our current\nL167: research interest.6,7\nL168: \nL169: In order to better understand the microscopic mecha-\nL170: \nL171: nisms of the MCE in simple compounds we chose to study\nL172: \nL173: compounds of the family R Al 2. T o describe them and calcu-\nL174: \nL175: late the MCE along the crystallographic directions, we used\nL176: \nL177: a model that includes the exchange and crystalline electric-\nL178: \nL179: ﬁeld 共 CEF 兲 interactions, and applied it to the compounds\nL180: \nL181: with R=Pr or Nd. This work describes the theoretical results\nL182: \nL183: and compares them with available experimental data.\nL184: Also, an optimum molar fraction of PrAl 2 and NdAl 2\nL185: \nL186: was determined for the assembling of a composite to be used\nL187: \nL188: as an active magnetic resonator 共 AMR 兲 at low temperatures.\nL189: \nL190: The MCE for the composite was calculated and measured,\nL191: \nL192: and the results are discussed in the framework of an opti-\nL193: \nL194: mum regeneration Ericsson cycle.\nL195: \nL196: THEORETICAL MODEL\nL197: \nL198: The PrAl 2 and NdAl 2 compounds crystallize in the cubic\nL199: \nL200: MgCu 2-type structure, and their magnetism is described us-\nL201: ing a magnetic Hamiltonian that includes the CEF and the\nL202: \nL203: Zeeman-exchange interaction terms.\nL204: \nL205: H\nL206: \nL207: &\nL208: \nL209: =W\nL210: \nL211: 冋\nL212: \nL213: X\nL214: \nL215: F 4 共 O 4\nL216: \nL217: 0+5 O 4\nL218: \nL219: 4 兲+共 1−兩 X 兩兲\nL220: \nL221: F 6 共 O 6\nL222: \nL223: 0−2 1 O 6\nL224: \nL225: 4 兲\nL226: \nL227: 册\nL228: \nL229: −g\nL230: \nL231: ␮\nL232: \nL233: B 共 B m\nL234: \nL235: x J x+B m\nL236: \nL237: y J y+B m\nL238: \nL239: z J z 兲,共 1 兲\nL240: \nL241: where\nL242: \nL243: B m\nL244: \nL245: x=B cos 共\nL246: \nL247: ␣\nL248: \nL249: 兲+␭M x,共 2 兲\nL250: \nL251: B m\nL252: \nL253: y=B cos 共\nL254: \nL255: ␤\nL256: \nL257: 兲+␭M y,共 3 兲\nL258: \nL259: B m\nL260: \nL261: z=B cos 共\nL262: \nL263: ␥\nL264: \nL265: 兲+␭M z,共 4 兲\nL266: \nL267: M\nL268: \nL269: ␩\nL270: \nL271: =\nL272: \nL273: 冋\nL274: \nL275: 兺\nL276: \nL277: n exp\nL278: \nL279: 冉\nL280: \nL281: −e n\nL282: \nL283: k B T\nL284: \nL285: 冊\nL286: \nL287: 册\nL288: \nL289: −1 兺\nL290: \nL291: n 具 e n 兩 M\nL292: \nL293: ␩\nL294: \nL295: 兩 e n 典 exp\nL296: \nL297: 冉\nL298: \nL299: −e n\nL300: \nL301: k B T\nL302: \nL303: 冊\nL304: \nL305: .\nL306: \nL307: 共 5 兲\nL308: \nL309: The cos 共\nL310: \nL311: ␣\nL312: \nL313: 兲, cos 共\nL314: \nL315: ␤\nL316: 兲, and cos 共\nL317: \nL318: ␥\nL319: \nL320: 兲 are the direction cosines\nL321: \nL322: of the external magnetic ﬁeld with respect to the crystallo-\nL323: \nL324: graphic axis. The component of the magnetization in the di-\nL325: \nL326: rection of the applied magnetic ﬁeld, M\nL327: \nL328: ␩\nL329: \nL330: , is obtained using\nL331: \nL332: Eqs. 共 2 兲–共 4 兲, together with relation 共 5 兲 using self-consistent\nL333: \nL334: calculations. The magnetic, lattice, and electronic entropies\nL335: \nL336: are given by 8,9\nL337: \nL338: S mag 共 B,T 兲=R\nL339: \nL340: 再\nL341: \nL342: ln\nL343: \nL344: 冋\nL345: \nL346: 兺\nL347: \nL348: n exp\nL349: \nL350: 冉\nL351: \nL352: −e n\nL353: \nL354: k B T\nL355: \nL356: 冊\nL357: \nL358: 册\nL359: \nL360: +具 E 典\nL361: \nL362: k B T\nL363: \nL364: 冎\nL365: \nL366: ,共 6 兲\nL367: a 兲 Author to whom correspondence should be addressed; electronic mail:\nL368: \nL369: magnus@iﬁ.unicamp.br\nL370: \nL371: JOURNAL OF APPLIED PHYSICS 97, 083905 共 2005 兲\nL372: \nL373: 0021-8979/2005/97 共 8 兲/083905/5/$22.50 © 2005 American Institute of Physics 97, 083905-1\nL374: \nL375: Downloaded 19 May 2005 to 143.106.128.160. Redistribution subject to AIP license or copyright, see http://jap.aip.org/jap/copyright.jsp\nL376: \nL377: S latt =−3 R ln\nL378: \nL379: 冋\nL380: \nL381: 1 − exp\nL382: \nL383: 冉\nL384: \nL385: −T D\nL386: \nL387: T\nL388: \nL389: 冊\nL390: \nL391: 册\nL392: \nL393: +1 2 R\nL394: \nL395: 冉\nL396: \nL397: T\nL398: \nL399: T D\nL400: \nL401: 冊\nL402: \nL403: 3\nL404: \nL405: 冕\nL406: \nL407: 0\nL408: \nL409: T D/T x 3 dx\nL410: \nL411: exp 共 x 兲−1,共 7 兲\nL412: \nL413: S el =\nL414: \nL415: ␥\nL416: ¯\nL417: \nL418: T,共 8 兲\nL419: \nL420: where T D is the effective Debye temperature and\nL421: \nL422: ␥\nL423: \nL424: ¯\nL425: \nL426: is the\nL427: \nL428: electronic heat-capacity coefﬁcient.\nL429: \nL430: EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURE\nL431: \nL432: Polycrystalline samples were prepared by arc-melting\nL433: \nL434: the appropriate mixtures of the elements in high-purity argon\nL435: \nL436: atmosphere on a water-cooled copper hearth. W e repeated\nL437: \nL438: the melting process three times in order to obtain high ho-\nL439: \nL440: mogeneity samples. The purity of the starting materials was\nL441: \nL442: 99.99% for aluminum and 99.9% for the rare-earth metals.\nL443: The samples were characterized using metallographic exami-\nL444: \nL445: nation, x-ray powder- diffraction measurements with Cu K\nL446: \nL447: ␣\nL448: \nL449: radiation, and magnetic measurements using a commercial\nL450: \nL451: Quantum Design superconducting quantum interference de-\nL452: \nL453: vice 共 SQUID 兲 7-T dc magnetometer in the temperature\nL454: \nL455: ranges of interest. The NdAl 2 and PrAl 2 composite was ob-\nL456: \nL457: tained simply by manually grinding the materials into a ﬁne\nL458: \nL459: powder and mixing the calculated amounts of the com-\nL460: \nL461: pounds.\nL462: W e obtained ⌬S mag 共 T 兲 versus temperature curves from\nL463: \nL464: the magnetization versus temperature experimental data 共 us-\nL465: \nL466: ing the Maxwell relation 兲 for these compounds.\nL467: \nL468: RESULTS AND DISCUSSIONS\nL469: \nL470: The x-ray powder-diffraction measurements for both\nL471: \nL472: compounds 共 Fig. 1 兲 show the presence only of the desired\nL473: \nL474: C-15 pure phase, and this is conﬁrmed by metallographic\nL475: \nL476: analyses.\nL477: \nL478: Figure 2 shows the magnetization as a function of tem-\nL479: \nL480: perature for the compounds PrAl 2 and NdAl 2. W e note that\nL481: the magnetic transition temperatures are 32.5 and 77 K, re-\nL482: \nL483: spectively, in close agreement with literature data, where the\nL484: \nL485: transition temperatures are reported to lie in the range from\nL486: \nL487: 31 up to 38.5 K for PrAl 2 共 Refs. 10–13 兲 and in the range\nL488: \nL489: from 65 up to 80 K for NdAl 2.14,15\nL490: \nL491: The model parameters used for PrAl 2 and NdAl 2 were\nL492: \nL493: W=−0.329 meV, X=0.739, ␭=256.3 T 2/meV, and W\nL494: \nL495: =0.16 meV, X=−0.37, ␭=418.1 T 2/meV, respectively, taken\nL496: \nL497: from Ref. 16. The effective Debye temperature was taken\nL498: from the nonmagnetic and isostructural systems LaAl 2 and\nL499: \nL500: LuAl 2 using an interpolation procedure.10 The electronic\nL501: \nL502: heat-capacity coefﬁcient,\nL503: \nL504: ␥\nL505: \nL506: ¯\nL507: \nL508: =5.4 mJ mol−1 K−2, was assumed\nL509: \nL510: to be equal to that of the nonmagnetic compound LuAl 2.8\nL511: \nL512: Figures 3 and 4 show the temperature dependence of the\nL513: \nL514: theoretical and experimental isothermal ⌬S mag=S 共 H=0 兲\nL515: \nL516: −S 共 H⫽0 兲, in PrAl 2 and NdAl 2 compounds, respectively, for\nL517: \nL518: a magnetic-ﬁeld change from 0 to 5 T. The solid lines rep-\nL519: resent the theoretical results and the open circles show the\nL520: \nL521: experimental data. The ⌬S mag maximum values occur at the\nL522: \nL523: Curie temperatures since at this temperature an applied ﬁeld\nL524: \nL525: has a maximum reduction effect on the magnetic entropy in\nL526: \nL527: normal ferromagnetic systems. For PrAl 2, our theoretical re-\nL528: \nL529: sults are in excellent agreement with the experiment, consid-\nL530: \nL531: ering that no parameter adjustment was performed in order to\nL532: \nL533: FIG. 1. X-ray powder-diffraction patterns for PrAl 2 and NdAl 2 compounds\nL534: 共 the numbers in parenthes’s are the planes Miller indices 兲.FIG. 2. Magnetization of PrAl 2 and NdAl 2 compounds as a function of\nL535: \nL536: temperature, with an applied magnetic ﬁeld H=100 Oe.\nL537: \nL538: FIG. 3. Temperature dependence of ⌬S mag in PrAl 2 for a magnetic-ﬁeld\nL539: \nL540: change from 0 to 5 T calculated for the easy magnetic direction 具 001 典 and\nL541: \nL542: measured in a polycrystalline sample. The solid line represents the theoret-\nL543: \nL544: ical results using the model parameters given. The open circles show the\nL545: \nL546: experimental data.\nL547: 083905-2 Carvalho\nL548: \nL549: et al.\nL550: \nL551: J. Appl. Phys. 97, 083905 共 2005 兲\nL552: \nL553: Downloaded 19 May 2005 to 143.106.128.160. Redistribution subject to AIP license or copyright, see http://jap.aip.org/jap/copyright.jsp\nL554: \nL555: obtain a better ﬁt to the data. On the other hand, for NdAl 2\nL556: \nL557: our theoretical results do not agree with the experimental\nL558: \nL559: ones 共 see the dotted line 兲. The Curie temperature experimen-\nL560: \nL561: tally observed in our sample 共 T C⬃77 K 兲 differs from the\nL562: literature value 共 T C⬃65 K 兲, to which the value of the ex-\nL563: \nL564: change parameter, ␭=418.1 T 2/meV, was ascribed.16 In order\nL565: \nL566: to adjust the experimental Curie temperature for our sample,\nL567: \nL568: the exchange parameter ␭=491 T 2/meV was determined for\nL569: \nL570: NdAl 2.\nL571: \nL572: Figure 5 shows the magnetocaloric effect, ⌬S mag and\nL573: \nL574: ⌬T ad, calculated for the three main crystallographic cubic\nL575: \nL576: directions, namely, 具 001 典,具 111 典, and 具 110 典 in PrAl 2. Figure 6\nL577: \nL578: shows the same data for NdAl 2. The dotted and solid lines\nL579: represent the magnetocaloric effect considering the external\nL580: \nL581: magnetic ﬁeld from 0 to 2 T and from 0 to 5 T, respectively.\nL582: \nL583: In both PrAl 2 and NdAl 2 compounds the easy and hard mag-\nL584: \nL585: netic directions are, respectively, 具 001 典 and 具 111 典, which\nL586: \nL587: were determined by considering the magnetization versus\nL588: \nL589: magnetic-ﬁeld curves, data not presented in this work. Also,\nL590: \nL591: the free-energy analysis conﬁrms these easy directions for\nL592: \nL593: both compounds. W e note that, applying the magnetic ﬁeld in\nL594: the PrAl 2 easy magnetic direction, much heat can be ex-\nL595: \nL596: tracted over the whole temperature range, as expected. Nev-\nL597: \nL598: ertheless, in the NdAl 2 this is not true for the whole tempera-\nL599: \nL600: ture range. The small and anomalous peak in the ⌬T ad vs T\nL601: \nL602: curve appearing at about T=12.8 K increases the magneto-\nL603: \nL604: caloric effect in the 具 110 典 crystallographic direction 共⌬T ad\nL605: \nL606: =0.7 K 兲 followed by the lower values for ﬁeld in the hard\nL607: \nL608: direction 具 111 典共⌬T ad=0.45 K 兲 and in the easy direction\nL609: 具 001 典共⌬T ad=0.3 K 兲. The small peak comes from the crystal\nL610: \nL611: electric-ﬁeld anisotropy considered in our model calculation,\nL612: \nL613: since this anomalous peak disappears when we take W=0\nL614: \nL615: 共 no crystal- ﬁeld interaction 兲. Note that in PrAl 2, below the\nL616: \nL617: critical temperature 共 T⬃32.5 K 兲, an anomalous peak also\nL618: \nL619: emerges for ﬁeld applied along the hard direction. This peak\nL620: \nL621: could not be associated with a high density of crystal- ﬁeld\nL622: \nL623: states as in the case of 共 Dy 1−z Er z 兲 Al 2, in which a similar\nL624: anomalous peak was experimentally observed and theoreti-\nL625: \nL626: cally understood for concentrations z=0.5 and z=0.3.17 The\nL627: \nL628: anomalous magnetocaloric effect theoretically predicted for\nL629: \nL630: PrAl 2 and NdAl 2 in the hard directions requires further ex-\nL631: \nL632: perimental investigations using single crystals of these com-\nL633: \nL634: pounds. This study is in progress.\nL635: \nL636: The Ericsson cycle is the appropriate one to be used in\nL637: \nL638: magnetic refrigerator when the ferromagnetic material pre-\nL639: sents Curie temperature above 20 K, since the lattice en-\nL640: \nL641: tropy, which works as a heat load, grows considerably in\nL642: \nL643: comparison with the magnetic entropy.1,18 The peak observed\nL644: \nL645: and calculated in the ⌬S mag vs T curves is not desirable for a\nL646: \nL647: magnetic refrigerant material to operate a magnetic refrigera-\nL648: \nL649: tor using an Ericsson cycle. An ideal Ericsson cycle employs\nL650: \nL651: a constant value for the ⌬S mag in the temperature range of\nL652: \nL653: refrigeration, which is necessary for the improvement of the\nL654: regenerative processes.19 Considering the two compounds in-\nL655: \nL656: FIG. 4. T emperature dependence of ⌬S mag in NdAl 2 for a magnetic ﬁeld\nL657: \nL658: change from 0 to 5 T calculated for the easy magnetic direction 具 001 典 and\nL659: \nL660: measured in a polycrystalline sample. The solid line represents the theoret-\nL661: \nL662: ical results using the model parameters given and the dotted line was cal-\nL663: \nL664: culated using the CEF and exchange parameters from Ref. 7. The open\nL665: \nL666: circles show the experimental data.\nL667: \nL668: FIG. 5. Temperature dependence of\nL669: ⌬S mag and ⌬T ad for a magnetic-ﬁeld\nL670: \nL671: change from 0 to 2 T and 0 to 5 T\nL672: \nL673: calculated in the three main crystallo-\nL674: \nL675: graphic cubic directions for the PrAl 2\nL676: \nL677: compound.\nL678: \nL679: 083905-3 Carvalho\nL680: \nL681: et al.\nL682: \nL683: J. Appl. Phys. 97, 083905 共 2005 兲\nL684: \nL685: Downloaded 19 May 2005 to 143.106.128.160. Redistribution subject to AIP license or copyright, see http://jap.aip.org/jap/copyright.jsp\nL686: \nL687: vestigated in this work to be used as low-temperature mag-\nL688: \nL689: netic regenerators, we calculated the optimum molar propor-\nL690: tions that a composite material, formed by PrAl 2 and NdAl 2,\nL691: \nL692: should present in order to produce ⌬S mag as constant as pos-\nL693: \nL694: sible between the cold and hot temperatures. Figure 7 shows\nL695: \nL696: the calculated ⌬S mag\nL697: \nL698: composite=y⌬S mag\nL699: \nL700: PrAl 2+共 1−y 兲⌬S mag\nL701: \nL702: NdAl 2 versus\nL703: \nL704: temperature. The molar proportion value obtained for the\nL705: \nL706: easy direction was y=0.290 for an external magnetic-ﬁeld\nL707: \nL708: change from 0 to 5 T and y=0.282 when the magnetic ﬁeld\nL709: \nL710: was varied from 0 to 2 T. The used optimum numerical ap-\nL711: proach can be found in Ref. 20, which considers optimum\nL712: \nL713: proportions that a composite material must present between\nL714: \nL715: the low and high critical Curie temperatures. For other tem-\nL716: \nL717: perature intervals, different y values can be determined to\nL718: \nL719: realize the regenerative balance.21 The dotted lines in Fig. 7\nL720: \nL721: represent the calculated ⌬S mag vs T for the individual PrAl 2\nL722: \nL723: and NdAl 2 compounds for the above-considered magnetic-\nL724: \nL725: ﬁeld changes. The solid lines give the ⌬S mag\nL726: \nL727: composite vs T for the\nL728: same considered magnetic-ﬁeld changes. It is worth noticing\nL729: \nL730: that the y value is weakly dependent on the magnetic-ﬁeld\nL731: \nL732: changes considered 共 at about 2.8%兲.\nL733: \nL734: From the theoretical curves that ﬁt the experimental data\nL735: \nL736: for PrAl 2 and NdAl 2 compounds 共 Figs. 3 and 4, solid lines 兲,\nL737: \nL738: we calculated the best y value 共 y=0.292 兲 for the composite\nL739: \nL740: to be prepared. This composite was prepared from the com-\nL741: \nL742: pounds in powder form. These powders were mixed to pro-\nL743: duce a homogeneous mixture. Then, we performed the mag-\nL744: \nL745: netic measurements and calculated the isothermal magnetic\nL746: \nL747: entropy change 共⌬S mag 兲 and recalculated the ⌬S mag theoreti-\nL748: \nL749: cal curve 共 Fig. 8 兲, using the same parameters used for ﬁtting\nL750: \nL751: the PrAl 2 and NdAl 2 compounds curves. W e see that there is\nL752: \nL753: a good agreement between the model and experiment, as\nL754: \nL755: observed for the individual MCE’s. The dif ference between\nL756: \nL757: the experimental and theoretical results can be ascribed to\nL758: the fact that calculations were done for single crystals in the\nL759: \nL760: easy direction, whereas the measurements were performed\nL761: \nL762: on a polycrystalline composite.\nL763: \nL764: CONCLUSIONS\nL765: \nL766: In this paper, theoretical and experimental investigations\nL767: \nL768: were performed in the PrAl 2 and NdAl 2 magnetocaloric po-\nL769: \nL770: FIG. 6. Temperature dependence of\nL771: \nL772: ⌬S mag and ⌬T ad for a magnetic ﬁeld\nL773: \nL774: change from 0 to 2 T and 0 to 5 T\nL775: \nL776: calculated in the three main crystallo-\nL777: \nL778: graphic cubic directions for the NdAl 2\nL779: \nL780: compound.\nL781: FIG. 7. Temperature dependence of ⌬S mag\nL782: \nL783: composite=y⌬S mag\nL784: \nL785: PrAl 2+共 1−y 兲⌬S mag\nL786: \nL787: NdAl 2 for\nL788: \nL789: a magnetic-ﬁeld change from 0 to 2 T, using y=0.282, and 0 to 5 T 共 the\nL790: \nL791: upper curve 兲, using y=0.290. The dotted lines represent ⌬S mag\nL792: \nL793: PrAl 2 and ⌬S mag\nL794: \nL795: NdAl 2\nL796: \nL797: vs temperature for the two considered magnetic- ﬁeld changes. FIG. 8. Theoretical and experimental magnetocaloric effect 共⌬S mag 兲 for the\nL798: \nL799: y 共 PrAl 2 兲+共 1−y 兲共 NdAl 2 兲 composite, with y=0.292.\nL800: \nL801: 083905-4 Carvalho\nL802: \nL803: et al.\nL804: J. Appl. Phys. 97, 083905 共 2005 兲\nL805: \nL806: Downloaded 19 May 2005 to 143.106.128.160. Redistribution subject to AIP license or copyright, see http://jap.aip.org/jap/copyright.jsp\nL807: \nL808: tentials. The strong dependence of the magnetocaloric poten-\nL809: \nL810: tials on the choice of applied magnetic-ﬁeld direction was\nL811: \nL812: ﬁrst predicted using a Hamiltonian that takes into account the\nL813: \nL814: CEF anisotropy.The comparison between our theoretical and\nL815: \nL816: experimental results for the temperature dependence of\nL817: ⌬S mag in PrAl 2 and NdAl 2 for a magnetic-ﬁeld change from\nL818: \nL819: 0 to 5 T showed good agreement. Also, we determined theo-\nL820: \nL821: retically the optimum molar fraction of these two compounds\nL822: \nL823: in order to form a composite to be considered as an optimal\nL824: \nL825: refrigerant material working in an Ericsson cycle in the tem-\nL826: \nL827: perature interval deﬁned by their transition temperatures. The\nL828: \nL829: criterion to obtain the optimal molar ratios is from Ref. 20.\nL830: \nL831: The molar fraction is weakly dependent on the two\nL832: magnetic-ﬁeld changes 共 0–2 T an d 0–5 T 兲 considered in\nL833: \nL834: this work. Experiment with a powdered composite built in\nL835: \nL836: accordance with the theoretical calculated proportion showed\nL837: \nL838: a good agreement with the calculations.\nL839: \nL840: Presently, we are growing single crystals in order to per-\nL841: \nL842: form magnetic measurements along the crystallographic di-\nL843: \nL844: rections to verify the theoretical predictions of this model.\nL845: \nL846: ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\nL847: \n"